              {
                "text": "**Analyzing Input & Output**\n\nI've been dissecting the relationship between the provided input and output grids. Taking Example 1 as a guide, I see clear height changes reflected in the output. The heights of the numbers seem to be the focus of the modifications. It seems the output is a modified version of the input, with the input heights being changed to generate the outputs.\n\n\n**Inverting Block Order**\n\nI've made a breakthrough. I examined row heights of Example 1, noting the block order flips vertically in the output. Testing this inversion hypothesis with Example 2 reveals it holds true there, too. Each vertical block in the input inverts its position in the output, while maintaining its horizontal coordinates. This looks like the core transformation!\n\n\n**Refining Block Swaps**\n\nI've been examining the block-swapping behavior closely. My focus is on Example 3 and comparing the input/output block orders. I observe a swap between the first and last blocks in each example. Initially, I thought all the blocks were inverted. However, further testing revealed the block order swaps the top and bottom block only, while the internal blocks maintain their order. The change from inverting blocks to only swapping top and bottom blocks is a key refinement.\n\n\n",
